1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,280
What was the reasoning behind this call into an investigation?

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:09,400
You know, I have followed the coverage of this project, and I hadn't followed it closely

3
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,140
as it was being awarded.

4
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:16,700
To listen to the facts, you have a total bait and switch.

5
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:21,500
This was advertised for bid at $10 million in subsidies.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:26,920
Now we're over 80, and you're building low income housing units and mixed income housing

7
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:34,880
units to the tune of $600,000, $610,000 apiece in subsidy alone.

8
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:40,960
This seems like a radical waste of money, and this is a sought after piece of property.

9
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:45,920
If the circumstances as they are today had been advertised, I think you would have had

10
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:53,080
a line of local developers that would have use their imagination and bid on a great project.

11
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:58,600
Instead, we have an out-of-town firm that's won this, and they have been showered with

12
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:05,760
public subsidies between a 30-year pilot, a $30 million in federal tax credits that

13
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:10,720
are supposed to be geared towards low income housing, as well as just a pile of state money

14
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:11,720
as well.

15
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:16,280
If a project needs this to be that viable, maybe it's not all that's cracked up to be

16
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:17,560
in the first place.

17
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:23,160
So I think the only way, after listening to business leaders, but also just the investigative

18
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:27,500
coverage between all the different local media, it looks like it's very clear that the state

19
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:30,360
violated its own policies on procurement.

20
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:35,720
And I asked the question of the inspector general of the Department of Housing and Urban

21
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:37,400
Development to review this.

22
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:40,000
Is this an appropriate use of federal tax credits?

23
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,120
These are a finite supply.

24
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,880
There's not enough for every project.

25
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:49,800
And yes, this is a large, high-profile deal downtown.

26
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:55,180
But these projects are for everyone and for every district, and there's a lot of places

27
00:01:55,180 --> 00:02:01,000
throughout Western Europe and the southern tier where we have just as big of a housing

28
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,280
need and those low-income tax credits, are they best used on something of this grand

29
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:08,280
stature?

30
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:10,760
It doesn't pass the smell test to them.

31
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:17,800
You think of that plot of land on the waterfront, part of this effort to bring the waterfront

32
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:25,280
to be this economic force, and then you hear they get 30 years of a tax break.

33
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:26,560
How's that helping Western New York?

34
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:30,320
How's that helping Buffalo to not pumping money back into the economy?

35
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:35,040
The idea that we're going to be building low-income housing to the tune of almost a million dollars

36
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:38,120
a unit is a staggering sum of money.

37
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:43,240
Think of the home you can build for a million dollars, let alone an apartment unit.

38
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:50,520
This just doesn't pass the smell test as to be the best efficient use of a finite supply

39
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:54,880
of federal tax credits that are available for low-income housing.

40
00:02:54,880 --> 00:03:00,120
And I understand you want the city to be affordable for everybody, but there seems to be a lot

41
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:05,800
of better ways that you could address housing concerns in the city and all of the surrounding

42
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:10,440
communities, rather than just create one high-profile project that sucks up all of

43
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:11,440
the tax dollars.

44
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:17,980
Yeah, when you talk about these tax credits, what are the meaning of them and how are they

45
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,920
distributed between districts?

46
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:26,000
The state has large input on this, and it's certain amounts of these tax credits made

47
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,720
available to them, and the state has decided that this, when it was advertised for bid

48
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:38,800
at a $10 million subsidy, which there's sticker shock when you say $10 million in tax credits.

49
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:43,480
When you say that now you're going to get 30 in federal and 50 in state, now you're

50
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:50,440
just in an exorbitant amount of tax revenue that is going in to build these housing units

51
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:54,360
with very little retail, and it strikes me that they should really be looking for more

52
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:59,920
destinations to create a lot more commerce downtown.

53
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:03,480
That would be the goal, and it sounded like that was the goal in the initial proposal.

54
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:07,960
I mean, have you ever seen a project that took this drastic of a move?

55
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:14,120
I mean, originally it was going to be 75% market rate, now it's 75% affordable housing.

56
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:15,920
Have you ever seen something like that before?

57
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:22,480
Well, because it's gained the programs to make it viable, and that's the unfortunate

58
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:31,000
part of a lot of the gimmickry that is available to developers, that it's clear that they came

59
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,200
back and said, the numbers don't work, you've got to recalculate.

60
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:38,800
But if they're going to recalculate and they're going to put 80 million bucks up of hoodies

61
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:42,720
from the taxpayers, and let's face it, folks, it's your money, they should let everybody

62
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:43,800
reinvent the thing.

63
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:47,200
Why is this going to an out-of-town developer from Philadelphia?

64
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:52,040
No one can seem to answer that, and at $10 million, it may not have been as attractive.

65
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:57,640
But something tells me there'd be a lot of interested parties for a lot less than $80

66
00:04:57,640 --> 00:05:00,680
million in taxpayer giveaways.

67
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:03,600
So I'm not accusing anyone of any crime.

68
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:08,880
I've asked the Inspector General of the Department of Housing and Urban Development to determine

69
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:13,960
if the law was followed, and if this is an appropriate and efficient use of these tax

70
00:05:13,960 --> 00:05:14,960
credits.

71
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:19,040
And those are the questions that need to be answered, is should the federal government

72
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:24,920
be a partner in this the way it was executed, or should it be re-bid from the beginning?

73
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:33,480
Now, obviously, your goal is to make sure, like you said, that the rules are being followed

74
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:38,440
and these credits are being where they need to go, and obviously you want credits for

75
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:45,360
your district, but are you surprised you're the only elected official who's actually responding

76
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:48,640
to the complaints of business leaders in the city of Buffalo?

77
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:51,600
Perhaps it's because I don't represent the city proper.

78
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:56,160
I mean, let's face it, business leaders, they get a lot of pressure from people in power

79
00:05:56,160 --> 00:05:57,360
to not ask questions.

80
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:02,840
I don't have to worry about the leaders of the city, but I am a member of the House Oversight

81
00:06:02,840 --> 00:06:03,840
Committee.

82
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:06,760
I have oversight over the entire federal government in that respect.

83
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:12,680
We can ask these questions, and this deserves just a little more scrutiny before we start

84
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:16,640
writing blank checks for, you know, giant giveaways.

85
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:21,200
And, you know, there's a lot of business leaders that have come forward and said, this isn't

86
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:22,200
on the up and up.

87
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:27,520
And I think there's been some excellent reporting that I've watched and read and listened to

88
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:29,040
that have laid these facts out.

89
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:30,040
They're very inconvenient.

90
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:37,200
I haven't accused any politician of doing anything wrong here, but this is a sour deal.

91
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:44,400
And if you're offering $80 million for a subsidy on a project like this, then they should re-bid

92
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:45,400
the whole thing.

93
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:50,480
It's up to the Inspector General to take some action and to take a look at whether or not

94
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:56,320
the state actually filed its own procurement laws, which is, I think, the most troubling

95
00:06:56,320 --> 00:07:02,160
part that I've seen is that the state completely violates policies from the way I understand

96
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:03,160
the law.

97
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:06,880
I don't represent myself as an attorney, but if they violated their process, they should

98
00:07:06,880 --> 00:07:08,680
restart their process.

99
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:09,680
Absolutely.

100
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:10,680
Absolutely.

101
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:11,680
No pushback on that.

102
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:18,160
I have to say, Congressman, I am surprised that there aren't members of city government

103
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:23,520
or even at the state level pushing back on this because I just don't see it in the best

104
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:27,680
interest of what they're trying to do on that plot of land.

105
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:28,960
For me, that's the biggest thing.

106
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:29,960
This is waterfront.

107
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:33,520
We've heard about making the best of the north odd block.

108
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:34,880
This isn't what they were sold.

109
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:39,520
And usually, if I'm given something that I wasn't sold, I take it for a return and start

110
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:40,720
from point A.

111
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:44,240
I think there's just a certain desperation to get something going.

112
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:50,440
And we shouldn't just settle for something when it's a raw meal for the taxpayers.

113
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:56,080
You have the opportunity to start from scratch, reopen the conversation, invite anyone to

114
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:58,520
come forward with a great business proposal.

115
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:02,240
I mean, what is a great destination that might look at downtown Buffalo?

116
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:05,320
You don't have many parcels like that.

117
00:08:05,320 --> 00:08:12,320
And to use that for what is looking more and more like just to subsidize the term I

118
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:17,440
keep reading is low and mixed income, I do agree there is an affordability crisis.

119
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:21,840
But people that are trying to get a solution to the affordability crisis don't necessarily

120
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:25,160
expect luxury apartment buildings in the core of downtown.

